INTRODUCTION 
In today’s world, digital literacy is something everyone needs. It basically means knowing how to use technology 
properly and safely. This project was part of our Digital Literacy course, and it helped me understand how different 
digital tools actually work in real life.

As a Student Digital Ambassador, I completed five tasks like making an infographic, creating a portfolio, trying 
coding platforms, learning email etiquette, and understanding cybercrime. Each task gave me practical experience 
instead of just theory. It also made me realize how important it is to stay safe online and keep a professional image 
on the internet. Overall, this project was useful and helped me build skills that I’ll need later in academics and 
career. 


TASK 1 – INFOGRAPHIC 
For this task, I made an infographic on digital literacy using Canva. The main idea was to explain things in a 
simple way so that anyone can understand it easily. I focused on three parts: what digital literacy is, why it is 
important, safe internet practices, and email etiquette. 

I explained digital literacy as using technology properly and responsibly. I also added tools like Google Docs, 
GitHub, and Zoom since students use them a lot. I explained the importance of digital literacy using examples that 
students can understand and relate to. Along with that, I included safety tips like using strong passwords, not 
clicking random links, and keeping personal details safe. And I ended it with some important email etiquette that 
everyone should practice. 

While designing, I tried to keep it clean and not too messy by using icons and proper spacing. The hardest part was 
fitting everything in one page without making it look crowded. But overall, it turned out well. This task helped me 
improve how I present information and also made me a bit more creative. 


TASK 2 – PORTFOLIO 
In this task, I created my digital portfolio by making accounts on GitHub, LinkedIn, Kaggle and Stack Overflow. 
These platforms are useful for students to show their work and connect with others. 

On GitHub, I made my profile and added a README where I wrote about myself and my goals. On LinkedIn, I 
added my education details like my course and college. Kaggle was something new for me, and I explored how it is 
used for data science and practice. I explored the vast knowledge base, high-quality content and the efficiency with 
which answers are provided in Stack Overflow after creating my profile over there.

This task made me realize that having an online presence actually matters. In the future, I plan to upload my 
projects on GitHub, connect with people on LinkedIn, improve my skills using Kaggle, and deepen my 
understanding of software development using Stack Overflow. It felt like a small step now, but I know it will help 
later when applying for internships or jobs. 


TASK 3 – CODING & COLLABORATION PLATFORMS 
In Task 3, I explored coding and collaboration tools. First, I made an account on HackerRank and solved a basic 
coding problem. It was a bit challenging at first, but it helped me understand how to think logically while solving 
problems.

Then I created a Google Form called “Digital Literacy Awareness Quiz.” It had five questions, including MCQs 
and short answers. The purpose was to check how much people know about digital literacy and safety. I also linked 
it with Google Sheets to see responses easily. 

This task showed me how useful these tools are. HackerRank helps in improving coding skills, and Google Forms 
is really helpful for surveys and data collection. I can definitely use these in future assignments and projects.


TASK 4 – EMAIL ETIQUETTE & SOCIAL MEDIA CHECKLIST 
In this task, I learned how to write proper emails and use social media responsibly. I wrote two formal emails—one 
for asking an assignment extension and another for applying for an internship. I made sure to keep them clear and 
polite. 

I also made a checklist of what to do and what not to do on social media. It included things like being respectful, 
not sharing personal info, and avoiding fake news. 

This task made me understand that even small things like email format matter a lot. A badly written email can 
leave a wrong impression. So it’s important to communicate properly. 


TASK 5 – CYBERCRIME AWARENESS 
In this task, I learned about cybercrime, mainly phishing. It is when people try to fool you into giving your 
personal information through fake emails or websites. What surprised me was how easily people can fall for it. 
I made a checklist to stay safe, like not clicking unknown links, using strong passwords, and enabling two-factor 
authentication.

One habit I will change is being more careful online. Earlier, I didn’t think much before clicking links, but now I’ll 
always check properly. This task made me more aware of online risks. 


CONCLUSION 
This project helped me understand digital literacy in a practical way. I learned how to use different tools, write 
professional emails, and stay safe online. Each task taught me something useful. These skills will definitely help 
me in my studies and future career.


